<subsection xml:id="subsec-continuity-multivariate">
    <title>Continuity</title>

    <introduction>
        <p>
            Continuity for a function of several variables means that nearby inputs produce nearby outputs —
            exactly as in one dimension.
            The key new feature is that a point <m>\mathbf{x}_0 \in \R^n</m> can be approached from
            infinitely many directions, and the function must return the same limit along every approach path.
            We make this precise using sequences, because the same sequence definition used for
            single-variable limits carries over without change.
        </p>
    </introduction>

    <definition xml:id="def-sequence-limit-Rn">
        <title>Limit of a Sequence in <m>\R^n</m></title>
        <statement>
            <p>
                Let <m>(\mathbf{x}_m)_{m \in \N}</m> be a sequence in <m>\R^n</m>
                and let <m>\mathbf{x} \in \R^n</m>.
                We say that <m>(\mathbf{x}_m)</m> <term>converges to</term> <m>\mathbf{x}</m>,
                written
                <me>\lim_{m \to \infty} \mathbf{x}_m = \mathbf{x},</me>
                if for every <m>\varepsilon > 0</m> there exists <m>m_0</m> such that
                for all <m>m \ge m_0</m>:
                <me>\| \mathbf{x}_m - \mathbf{x} \| \lt \varepsilon.</me>
                Geometrically, <m>\mathbf{x}_m</m> eventually lies inside every open ball of radius <m>\varepsilon</m>
                centred at <m>\mathbf{x}</m>.
            </p>
            <figure xml:id="fig-sequence-in-r2">
                <caption>
                    Example of a sequence in <m>\R^2</m>: the points <m>\mathbf{x}_m=(1/m,1/m^2)</m> approach the limit <m>(0,0)</m>.
                </caption>
                <image width="58%">
                    <latex-image>
\begin{tikzpicture}[scale=1.1, >=stealth]
    \draw[->, gray!70] (-0.2,0) -- (3.1,0) node[right] {$x$};
    \draw[->, gray!70] (0,-0.2) -- (0,2.4) node[above] {$y$};
    \foreach \n/\x/\y in {1/2.4/1.9,2/1.2/0.48,3/0.8/0.21,4/0.6/0.12,5/0.48/0.08} {
        \fill[blue!70!black] (\x,\y) circle (0.06);
        \node[above right, blue!70!black] at (\x,\y) {\small $\mathbf{x}_{\n}$};
    }
    \draw[blue!60, thick, ->] (2.4,1.9) -- (1.2,0.48);
    \draw[blue!60, thick, ->] (1.2,0.48) -- (0.8,0.21);
    \draw[blue!60, thick, ->] (0.8,0.21) -- (0.6,0.12);
    \draw[blue!60, thick, ->] (0.6,0.12) -- (0.48,0.08);
    \fill[red!70!black] (0,0) circle (0.07);
    \node[below left, red!70!black] at (0,0) {\small $(0,0)$};
\end{tikzpicture}
                    </latex-image>
                </image>
            </figure>
        </statement>
    </definition>

    <remark xml:id="rem-sequence-componentwise">
        <title>Componentwise Convergence</title>
        <p>
            If <m>(\mathbf{x}_m) = (x_{m,1}, \ldots, x_{m,n})</m> is a sequence in <m>\R^n</m>, then it converges to <m>\mathbf{x}</m>
            if and only if each coordinate sequence converges:
            <m>x_{m,i} \to x_i</m> for <m>i = 1, \ldots, n</m>.
            This reduces questions about sequences in <m>\R^n</m> to <m>n</m> familiar
            one-dimensional problems.
        </p>
    </remark>

    <example xml:id="ex-sequence-Rn">
        <title>A Sequence in <m>\R^3</m></title>
        <p>
            The sequence
            <me>
                \mathbf{x}_n = \left(\frac{1}{n},\;
                2 - \frac{3n + 4n^2}{6n^3 + \ln n},\;
                e^{-n}\right)
            </me>
            in <m>\R^3</m> satisfies
            <m>\lim_{n \to \infty} \mathbf{x}_n = (0, 2, 0)</m>,
            since each coordinate converges: <m>1/n \to 0</m>,
            <m>(3n + 4n^2)/(6n^3 + \ln n) \to 0</m>, and <m>e^{-n} \to 0</m>.
        </p>
    </example>

    <definition xml:id="def-multivariate-limit">
        <title>Limit of a Multivariate Function</title>
        <statement>
            <p>
                Let <m>f \colon D_f \to \R</m>, and let <m>\mathbf{x}_0</m> be an accumulation point of
                <m>D_f</m>.
                We say that <m>f</m> has <term>limit</term> <m>z_0</m> at <m>\mathbf{x}_0</m> if for every
                sequence <m>(\mathbf{x}_m) \subset D_f \setminus \{\mathbf{x}_0\}</m> with
                <m>\lim_{m \to \infty} \mathbf{x}_m = \mathbf{x}_0</m>:
                <me>\lim_{m \to \infty} f(\mathbf{x}_m) = z_0.</me>
            </p>
            <p>
                For example, for <m>f(x,y)=x^2+y^2</m> and <m>\mathbf{x}_0=(0,0)</m>, every sequence approaching the origin has image values approaching <m>0</m>.
            </p>
            <figure xml:id="fig-limit-of-multivariate-function">
                <caption>
                    The function <m>f(x,y)=x^2+y^2</m> has limit <m>0</m> at <m>(0,0)</m>; different approach sequences in the plane still lead to the same function value limit.
                </caption>
                <image width="62%">
                    <latex-image>
\begin{tikzpicture}[scale=1.05, >=stealth]
    \draw[->, gray!70] (-2.2,0) -- (2.2,0) node[right] {$x$};
    \draw[->, gray!70] (0,-2.2) -- (0,2.2) node[above] {$y$};
    \foreach \r in {0.55,1.1,1.65} {
        \draw[blue!60!black, thick] (0,0) circle (\r);
    }
    \draw[red!70!black, very thick, ->] (1.8,0.9) -- (0.9,0.45);
    \draw[red!70!black, very thick, ->] (0.9,0.45) -- (0.45,0.225);
    \fill[red!70!black] (1.8,0.9) circle (0.05);
    \fill[red!70!black] (0.9,0.45) circle (0.05);
    \fill[red!70!black] (0.45,0.225) circle (0.05);
    \node[red!70!black] at (1.6,1.2) {\small $\mathbf{x}_m$};
    \draw[green!50!black, very thick, ->] (-1.6,1.6) -- (-0.8,0.8);
    \draw[green!50!black, very thick, ->] (-0.8,0.8) -- (-0.4,0.4);
    \fill[green!50!black] (-1.6,1.6) circle (0.05);
    \fill[green!50!black] (-0.8,0.8) circle (0.05);
    \fill[green!50!black] (-0.4,0.4) circle (0.05);
    \node[green!50!black] at (-1.45,1.95) {\small $\mathbf{y}_m$};
    \fill[black] (0,0) circle (0.06);
    \node[below left] at (0,0) {\small $(0,0)$};
\end{tikzpicture}
                    </latex-image>
                </image>
            </figure>
        </statement>
    </definition>

    <definition xml:id="def-multivariate-continuity">
        <title>Continuity of a Function of <m>n</m> Variables</title>
        <statement>
            <p>
                <ul>
                    <li>
                        <p>
                            A function <m>f \colon D_f \to \R</m> is
                            <term>continuous at <m>\mathbf{x}_0 \in D_f</m></term>
                            if for every sequence <m>(\mathbf{x}_m)</m> in <m>D_f</m> with
                            <m>\lim_{m \to \infty} \mathbf{x}_m = \mathbf{x}_0</m>:
                            <me>\lim_{m \to \infty} f(\mathbf{x}_m) = f(\mathbf{x}_0).</me>
                        </p>
                    </li>
                    <li>
                        <p>
                            A function is <term>continuous</term> if it is continuous at every point
                            of its domain.
                        </p>
                    </li>
                </ul>
            </p>
        </statement>
    </definition>

    <p>
        A key feature of multivariate functions is that continuity requires the same limit along
        every approach path, not only along coordinate axes.
        The next example shows how to prove discontinuity by finding two sequences approaching
        the same point with different limits.
    </p>

    <!-- ADDED: clarify a common pitfall when checking multivariable limits -->
    <remark xml:id="rem-path-test-warning">
        <title>How Path Tests Can and Cannot Be Used</title>
        <p>
            If two approach paths give different limiting values, the limit does not exist.
            However, checking only a few paths can never <em>prove</em> that a limit exists,
            because there are infinitely many possible ways to approach the point.
            To prove existence, one needs a general argument, for example by using continuity laws,
            an estimate, or a theorem.
        </p>
    </remark>

    <example xml:id="ex-discontinuity-rational">
        <title>Discontinuity via Two Approach Paths</title>
        <p>
            Analyse the continuity of
            <me>
                f(x,y) = \begin{cases}
                    \dfrac{-4x^3 y}{(x^2 + y^2)^2} &amp; (x,y) \ne (0,0), \\[4pt]
                    0 &amp; (x,y) = (0,0).
                \end{cases}
            </me>
        </p>
        <p>
            For <m>(x,y) \ne (0,0)</m>, the function is a ratio of polynomials with a non-vanishing
            denominator, hence continuous.
            At the origin we test two approach paths:
        </p>
        <p>
            <ul>
                <li>
                    <p>
                        Along the <m>x</m>-axis: <m>\mathbf{p}_n = (1/n, 0) \to (0,0)</m>.
                        <me>
                            f(\mathbf{p}_n)
                            = \frac{-4(1/n)^3 \cdot 0}{((1/n)^2 + 0)^2} = 0
                            \;\longrightarrow\; 0.
                        </me>
                    </p>
                </li>
                <li>
                    <p>
                        Along the diagonal: <m>\mathbf{q}_n = (1/n, 1/n) \to (0,0)</m>.
                        <me>
                            f(\mathbf{q}_n)
                            = \frac{-4(1/n)^3 \cdot (1/n)}{\left((1/n)^2 + (1/n)^2\right)^2}
                            = \frac{-4/n^4}{4/n^4} = -1
                            \;\longrightarrow\; -1.
                        </me>
                    </p>
                </li>
            </ul>
        </p>
        <p>
            Since the limits along the two paths differ,
            <m>\lim_{\mathbf{x} \to (0,0)} f(\mathbf{x})</m> does not exist,
            and <m>f</m> is not continuous at the origin.
        </p>
        <figure xml:id="fig-two-approach-paths">
            <caption>
                The two sequences <m>\mathbf{p}_n=(1/n,0)</m> and <m>\mathbf{q}_n=(1/n,1/n)</m> both approach the origin, but they produce different function value limits.
            </caption>
            <image width="58%">
                <latex-image>
\begin{tikzpicture}[scale=1.15, >=stealth]
    \draw[->, gray!70] (-0.2,0) -- (3.1,0) node[right] {$x$};
    \draw[->, gray!70] (0,-0.2) -- (0,3.1) node[above] {$y$};
    \draw[blue!70!black, very thick, ->] (2.5,0) -- (1.25,0);
    \draw[blue!70!black, very thick, ->] (1.25,0) -- (0.83,0);
    \draw[blue!70!black, very thick, ->] (0.83,0) -- (0.63,0);
    \foreach \x in {2.5,1.25,0.83,0.63} {
        \fill[blue!70!black] (\x,0) circle (0.05);
    }
    \node[blue!70!black] at (2.35,0.28) {\small $\mathbf{p}_n$};
    \draw[red!70!black, very thick, ->] (2.3,2.3) -- (1.15,1.15);
    \draw[red!70!black, very thick, ->] (1.15,1.15) -- (0.77,0.77);
    \draw[red!70!black, very thick, ->] (0.77,0.77) -- (0.58,0.58);
    \foreach \x in {2.3,1.15,0.77,0.58} {
        \fill[red!70!black] (\x,\x) circle (0.05);
    }
    \node[red!70!black] at (2.55,2.65) {\small $\mathbf{q}_n$};
    \draw[dashed, gray!60] (0,0) -- (2.7,2.7);
    \fill[black] (0,0) circle (0.06);
    \node[below left] at (0,0) {\small $(0,0)$};
\end{tikzpicture}
                </latex-image>
            </image>
        </figure>
    </example>

    <p>
        The following theorem shows that we can build many continuous functions of several variables from continuous functions of one variable by using sums, products, quotients, and compositions.
    </p>

    <theorem xml:id="thm-laws-of-continuity">
        <title>Laws of Continuity</title>
        <statement>
            <p>
                <ul>
                    <li>
                        <p>
                            <em>Constant functions</em>
                            <m>f(\mathbf{x}) = c</m> are continuous on <m>\R^n</m>.
                        </p>
                    </li>
                    <li>
                        <p>
                            <em>Coordinate functions</em>
                            <m>f_i(\mathbf{x}) = x_i</m> are continuous on <m>\R^n</m>.
                        </p>
                    </li>
                    <li>
                        <p>
                            If <m>f, g \colon D \subset \R^n \to \R</m> are continuous on <m>D</m>,
                            then so are <m>f + g</m>, <m>f - g</m>, and <m>f \cdot g</m>;
                            and <m>f/g</m> is continuous on <m>\{\mathbf{x} \in D \mid g(\mathbf{x}) \ne 0\}</m>.
                        </p>
                    </li>
                    <li>
                        <p>
                            <em>Compositions:</em> if
                            <m>f \colon D_f \subset \R^n \to \R</m> and
                            <m>g \colon D_g \subset \R \to \R</m> are continuous
                            with <m>f(D_f) \subset D_g</m>,
                            then <m>g \circ f</m> is continuous on <m>D_f</m>.
                        </p>
                    </li>
                </ul>
            </p>
        </statement>
        <proof>
            <p>
                We use the sequence criterion.
                Constant and coordinate functions preserve limits of convergent sequences immediately.
                If <m>\mathbf{x}_m \to \mathbf{x}_0</m> in <m>D</m> and
                <m>f(\mathbf{x}_m) \to f(\mathbf{x}_0)</m>, <m>g(\mathbf{x}_m) \to g(\mathbf{x}_0)</m>,
                then the usual one-variable limit laws imply
                <m>(f \pm g)(\mathbf{x}_m) \to (f \pm g)(\mathbf{x}_0)</m>,
                <m>(fg)(\mathbf{x}_m) \to (fg)(\mathbf{x}_0)</m>, and, provided
                <m>g(\mathbf{x}_0) \ne 0</m>, also
                <m>(f/g)(\mathbf{x}_m) \to (f/g)(\mathbf{x}_0)</m>.
                For compositions, <m>\mathbf{x}_m \to \mathbf{x}_0</m> implies
                <m>f(\mathbf{x}_m) \to f(\mathbf{x}_0)</m>, and continuity of <m>g</m> then gives
                <m>g(f(\mathbf{x}_m)) \to g(f(\mathbf{x}_0))</m>.
            </p>
        </proof>
    </theorem>

    <example xml:id="ex-continuity-product">
        <title>Proving Continuity via the Combination Laws</title>
        <p>
            Show that <m>f \colon \R^2 \to \R</m>,
            <m>f(x_1, x_2) = x_1 \cdot \sin(x_2)</m>, is continuous.
        </p>
        <p>
            Define auxiliary functions
            <m>f_1(x_1, x_2) = x_1</m> (coordinate function),
            <m>f_2(x_1, x_2) = x_2</m> (coordinate function), and
            <m>f_3(x) = \sin(x)</m> (a continuous real function).
            Then <m>f = f_1 \cdot (f_3 \circ f_2)</m>.
            By <xref ref="thm-laws-of-continuity"/>,
            the composition <m>f_3 \circ f_2</m> is continuous,
            and the product of two continuous functions is continuous,
            so <m>f</m> is continuous on <m>\R^2</m>.
        </p>
    </example>

</subsection>
